{"worker": "forge", "role": "code_drafter", "model": "codellama:13b", "task": "Draft tools/qsb_f25_worker_recruitment_loop.py. Requirements: (1) python3 stdlib only (no extra deps), (2) reads data/registries/qsb_f25_roster.json (workers list under 'workers' key, each has 'name' and 'role'), (3) per tick: pick one worker by round-robin, generate a short read-only audit task for their role (e.g. interviewer reads roster sizes, spawn_clerk counts qualified workers from qsb_certification_ledger.jsonl, onboarding_guide checks recent new hires), (4) append result to data/registr", "reply": "```python\nimport json\nimport time\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\n# Load worker roster from JSON file\nwith open(\"data/registries/qsb_f25_roster.json\") as f:\n    workers = json.load(f)[\"workers\"]\n\n# Define task functions for each role\ndef interviewer():\n    # Read roster sizes and return summary\n    return {\"interviewed\": len(workers)}\n\ndef spawn_clerk():\n    # Count qualified workers from certification ledger file\n    with open(\"data/registries/qsb_certification_ledger.jsonl\") as f:\n        qualified = sum([1 for line in f if \"qualified\" in line])\n    return {\"spawned\": qualified}\n\ndef onboarding_guide():\n    # Check recent new hires from tick log file\n    with open(\"data/registries/qsb_f25_tick_log.jsonl\") as f:\n        recent = [line for line in f if \"new_hire\" in line]\n    return {\"onboarded\": len(recent)}\n\n# Define main loop\nwhile True:\n    # Pick worker by round-robin\n    worker = workers[tick % len(workers)]\n\n    # Generate task for selected worker's role\n    task_func = {\n        \"interviewer\": interviewer,\n        \"spawn_clerk\": spawn_clerk,\n        \"onboarding_guide\": onboarding_guide,\n    }[worker[\"role\"]]\n    result = task_func()\n\n    # Append result to tick log file\n    with open(\"data/registries/qsb_f25_tick_log.jsonl\", \"a\") as f:\n        json.dump({\"ts\": time.time(), \"worker\": worker[\"name\"], \"role\": worker[\"role\"], \"task\": task_func.__name__, \"result_summary\": result}, f)\n\n    # Sleep for 60 seconds before next tick\n    time.sleep(60)\n```", "wall_s": 7.2, "ts": "2026-06-15T09:44:38Z"}
{"worker": "forge", "role": "code_drafter", "model": "codellama:13b", "task": "Write a complete Python file (~150 lines) for tools/qsb_kokoro_serve.py: a Flask HTTP server on port 8851 with one endpoint POST /tts that takes JSON {text, voice} and returns audio/wav bytes synthesized by kokoro_onnx (already installed). Use the kokoro_onnx.Kokoro class with default voices. Cache the Kokoro model in module scope. Add /health endpoint returning {ok: true, voices: [...]}. Use stdlib + flask only. Handle missing model file gracefully: fall back to 503 with {ok: false, error: 'mod", "reply": "```python\nimport os\nfrom flask import Flask, request, jsonify\nfrom kokoro_onnx import Kokoro\n\napp = Flask(__name__)\nkokoro = Kokoro()\nvoices = kokoro.list_voices()\n\n@app.route(\"/tts\", methods=[\"POST\"])\ndef tts():\n    data = request.get_json(force=True)\n    text, voice = data[\"text\"], data[\"voice\"]\n    if voice not in voices:\n        return jsonify({\"ok\": False, \"error\": f\"Invalid voice: {voice}\"}), 400\n    try:\n        audio = kokoro.synthesize(text, voice)\n    except Exception as e:\n        return jsonify({\"ok\": False, \"error\": str(e)}), 500\n    return audio, 200\n\n@app.route(\"/health\", methods=[\"GET\"])\ndef health():\n    return jsonify({\"ok\": True, \"voices\": voices}), 200\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    app.run(debug=True)\n```", "wall_s": 4.65, "ts": "2026-06-15T10:20:38Z"}
{"worker": "mira", "role": "reviewer", "model": "llama2:13b", "task": "Review whether running Arize Phoenix in this skyscraper makes sense. Phoenix can run as: (a) pip install arize-phoenix and run 'phoenix serve' to launch a local UI on :6006, OR (b) docker run on the same port. The skyscraper currently has tools/qsb_wren_local_agent.py, tools/qsb_provider_agent.py, and tools/qsb_telegram_receptionist.py making model calls. Question: is there a downside to running Phoenix in pip-only mode (no docker) and adding a OpenTelemetry decorator to those 3 call sites? Repl", "reply": "  Sure! Here's my review of running Arize Phoenix in this skyscraper:\n\nPros of running Phoenix in pip-only mode (no Docker):\n\n* Easier to set up and run locally without needing Docker\n* Faster development cycles with instant feedback from changes made to the code\n* Can be useful for debugging purposes, as it allows for more control over the environment and easier access to logs and debug information\n\nCons of running Phoenix in pip-only mode (no Docker):\n\n* Limited scalability and reliability compared to using Docker, which provides a more consistent and isolated environment\n* May not be suitable for production environments due to the lack of containerization and resource isolation\n* Could lead to conflicts with other processes or system resources if not properly configured and monitored\n\nVerdict: Revise. While running Phoenix in pip-only mode may be convenient for local development, it is not recommended for production environments due to the limitations mentioned above. Instead, consider using Docker to ensure consistency, scalability, and reliability. Additionally, adding OpenTelemetry decorators to the call sites can provide valuable insights into the performance and behavior of the system, but it should be done in conjunction with a more robust monitoring and logging setup.", "wall_s": 12.7, "ts": "2026-06-15T10:20:46Z"}
{"worker": "bram", "role": "triage", "model": "mistral:7b", "task": "Write tools/qsb_letta_bridge.py (~80 lines): a Python module that exposes 3 functions: (1) ensure_wren_agent() -> str returns agent_id, creates the Wren letta agent if it doesn't exist with persona='You are Wren, foreman of QSB Tower's apprentice crew. Honest, terse, propose-don't-execute.' and human='Ross \u2014 captain on a boat; lithium power; impatient with paper progress.' (2) wren_send(message: str, agent_id: str) -> str sends a message and returns the reply (3) wren_recall(query: str, agent_id", "reply": " Here's the Python module `tools/qsb_letta_bridge.py`:\n\n```python\nimport os\nfrom letta_sdk import LettaSDKClient\n\ndef ensure_wren_agent():\n    agent_id = \"Wren\"\n    if not os.path.exists(f\"{agent_id}.letta\"):\n        persona = 'You are Wren, foreman of QSB Tower\\'s apprentice crew. Honest, terse, propose-don\\'t-execute.'\n        human = 'Ross \u2014 captain on a boat; lithium power; impatient with paper progress.'\n        LettaSDKClient(server_url=\"http://localhost:8283\").create_agent(agent_id, persona, human)\n    return agent_id\n\ndef wren_send(message: str, agent_id: str):\n    client = LettaSDKClient(server_url=\"http://localhost:8283\")\n    reply = client.send_message(agent_id, message)\n    return reply\n\ndef wren_recall(query: str, agent_id: str):\n    client = LettaSDKClient(server_url=\"http://localhost:8283\")\n    response = client.search_memory(agent_id, query)\n    return response\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    agent_id = ensure_wren_agent()\n    print(wren_send('hello', agent_id))\n```\n\nThis module creates a Wren agent if it doesn't exist, sends a 'hello' message, and prints the reply. It uses the LettaSDKClient from the letta-sdk package to interact with the LettA service. The server URL defaults to `http://localhost:8283`.", "wall_s": 19.01, "ts": "2026-06-15T10:20:52Z"}
